// Package gitstate tells whether a project's build output may be cleaned as far as git is concerned: whether the project lies in a repository at all (RepoOf, which only looks for a .git entry), and if so whether the project folder has uncommitted changes and whether any of its build output folders holds files under version control (Checker.Check, which runs git through an execx.Runner).
//
// The answer errs on the side of keeping files. Only a project with no .git folder or file anywhere from it up to the root of its volume is "not a repository" (cleanup.GitNone); once a .git is found, every failure of git (not installed, a timeout, exit 128 such as "not a git repository" or "dubious ownership", any other error) is cleanup.GitUnknown, never GitNone. A project that git sees as one untracked folder of an enclosing repository (a repository whose work tree is the home folder, say) is GitUnknown too. Untracked entries inside build output folders that only ever hold generated content (Artifact.Generated: node_modules, target …) do not make a project dirty; untracked entries inside bin, obj, build or Pods do, since people keep their own files there.
//
// git runs with --no-optional-locks, core.fsmonitor off and quoted paths off, with GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0, GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 and LC_ALL=C, and without the GIT_DIR family of variables a calling shell or git hook may have set (execx.Command.Unset), so it always answers about the project's own repository. The package never writes to a repository.
package gitstate
